**Short version:** you've got two lazy-loading jobs in this project — the project gallery (target: shave ~350ms off initial load) and the modal popup (currently ~400ms of synchronous image loading on open). Same library, `lazysizes` v5.3.2, solves both. Below is the setup we've worked through, plus the simpler alternatives if you'd rather not carry a dependency.

---

## 1. Include lazysizes

CDN, preloaded and async so it doesn't block:

```html
<link rel="preload" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/lazysizes/5.3.2/lazysizes.min.js" as="script">
<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/lazysizes/5.3.2/lazysizes.min.js" async></script>
```

Or bundled:

```javascript
import 'lazysizes';
import 'lazysizes/plugins/parent-fit/ls.parent-fit'; // useful for responsive images in Bootstrap columns
```

## 2. Markup — Bootstrap grid + `data-src`

Real source goes in `data-src`; `src` carries a small low-resolution placeholder so you don't get layout shift. Keep `alt` on every image — it's the SEO half of the job.

```html
<div class="container">
  <div class="row">
    <div class="col-md-4 mb-4">
      <img class="w-100 lazyload" data-src="path/to/image1.jpg" src="placeholder.jpg" alt="Image 1">
    </div>
    <!-- … -->
  </div>
</div>
```

In card layouts, `card-img-top` and `lazyload` coexist fine:

```html
<div class="card">
  <img data-src="path/to/image.jpg" class="card-img-top lazyload" alt="Project Image">
  <div class="card-body">
    <h5 class="card-title">Project Title</h5>
    <p class="card-text">Project Description</p>
  </div>
</div>
```

## 3. Configuration and error handling

```javascript
const lazyLoadInstance = lazysizes.config({
  loadingClass: 'ls-loading',
  loadedClass:  'ls-loaded',
  lazyClass:    'lazy',
  errorClass:   'ls-error',
});

const galleryImages = document.querySelectorAll('.gallery-image');
galleryImages.forEach((image) => {
  image.classList.add('lazy');
  image.setAttribute('data-src', image.src);
  image.src = 'placeholder.jpg';
  image.removeAttribute('srcset');   // stops the browser fetching before lazysizes decides

  image.addEventListener('load',  () => console.log(`${image.src} loaded successfully.`));
  image.addEventListener('error', () => {
    console.error(`Failed to load ${image.src}.`);
    image.src = 'fallback.jpg';
    image.classList.remove('ls-error');
  });
});

document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  lazyLoadInstance.revalidate();
});
```

Two points worth flagging from your existing gallery code: you were missing `errorClass` and the `srcset` removal, and you had no `error` handler at all — a broken URL silently left a placeholder in the grid.

**Honest limit:** the `lazysizes.config({...})` / `.revalidate()` shape above is what we've been carrying through this thread, but verify it against the v5.3.2 docs before you ship — lazysizes primarily exposes a global config object rather than a config *function*, and if that's the case here the call will throw at init and you'll get zero lazy loading with no obvious symptom. Test with the network panel throttled and confirm images actually arrive on scroll, not on load.

## 4. The modal (the 400ms case)

Your `Modal.loadImages()` currently constructs a `new Image()` for every image on the page and fetches them all when the modal opens — that's the bottleneck, and it isn't lazy, it's just deferred-and-then-bulk. Give the modal images `data-src` + placeholder like the gallery, scope the selector to `#modal img` rather than `img`, and let lazysizes fetch them as they enter the modal's viewport. Bootstrap 5.3's `shown.bs.modal` event is the right moment to call `revalidate()` — the modal's contents have no layout until then, so anything measured earlier is measured against a hidden pane.

While you're in that markup, the accessibility attributes we discussed belong there too: `aria-labelledby` pointing at the modal title, `role="dialog"` on the container, `role="document"` on `.modal-dialog`, and `aria-label="Close"` on the close button.

---

## Simpler alternatives, if lazysizes is more than you need

- **Native:** `<img src="image.jpg" alt="Sample Image" loading="lazy">`. Zero dependencies, works for most cases, and is genuinely the easiest thing that could possibly work.
- **Intersection Observer:** ~15 lines, full control, no library — swap `data-src` into `src` on intersect and `unobserve` the element.
- **`lozad.js`:** `const observer = lozad('.lazy'); observer.observe();`

If your only goal is the ~350ms, start with `loading="lazy"` and measure. It may close the gap on its own, and it can't break.

